---
import { Picture } from "astro:assets";
import { heroPhoto } from "../data/gallery";
import { directionsUrl, primaryPhone } from "../data/contact";

/**
 * The entrance to the home page: the heading and what to do, beside a portrait of the
 * holding.
 *
 * ## What this replaced, and what went out with it
 *
 * Until September 2026 this was a full-bleed panorama with the `h1` lying on it over
 * `--hero-scrim`. That arrangement is gone, and three things went with it that are worth
 * knowing about, because each one used to be a rule somewhere:
 *
 * - **The scrim.** It was the only gradient in the design, and the only place on the site
 *   where contrast had to be measured in a browser rather than computed, because the
 *   background of the text was a photograph and the worst case was its brightest pixel. Text
 *   no longer lies on an image anywhere here. The measuring procedure is written up in
 *   `docs/inwentaryzacja.md` in case that ever changes - do not reinvent it, and do not
 *   estimate it offline.
 * - **The project's only media query.** The band switched 3:2 -> 3:1 at 700px because a
 *   panorama on a phone leaves a strip of photograph about as tall as a finger. A figure in a
 *   column has no such problem: `auto-fit` stacks it under the text and it keeps one ratio at
 *   every width. The project now has zero breakpoints.
 * - **The local `font-weight: 350` on the heading.** It was an optical correction for white
 *   type on a dark scrim, where a stroke reads heavier than the same stroke in ink on paper.
 *   On paper there is nothing to correct, so the override is gone - but the rendered weight
 *   does not change, because `global.css` sets 350 on every h1/h2/h3 anyway, on the owner's
 *   own instruction. What went away is a local exception that no longer had a reason, not a
 *   weight.
 *
 * ## The photograph, and why it is the wrong one
 *
 * **This frame is a placeholder for a photograph that does not exist yet, and it is the most
 * visible gap left on the site.** The figure is 4:5, and the holding has no free portrait
 * frame to put in it:
 *
 * - the 23 frames in `src/assets/gallery/` are all portrait and all sharp, but every one of
 *   them is a planting in the `compositions` collection, and that strip runs three sections
 *   below this one - the hero would reappear as a slide on the same page;
 * - `chrysanthemums/offer-0*` are portrait but only 736px wide, which is under one device
 *   pixel of this box on any modern screen;
 * - `pansies/offer-0*` are portrait and large, but they show March crates of pansies, and a
 *   year-round hero for a holding whose main season is April to June cannot be one product
 *   photographed in the wrong month.
 *
 * So `heroPhoto` stays, and it is cropped: it is 1448x1086 (4:3), a generated image rather
 * than a photograph from the session, and a 4:5 box shows about 60% of its width. The crop is
 * done once, by `object-fit` in CSS, and aimed by `object-position` - `width`/`height` below
 * carry the file's real ratio so that Astro only scales. Effective resolution across this box
 * is about 1.6x on a wide screen rather than 2x.
 *
 * **What to do when a portrait frame arrives:** swap `heroPhoto` in `src/data/gallery.ts`,
 * put the file's real dimensions in `width`/`height` here, and delete this section of the
 * comment. Nothing else changes. The request is on the owners' list in
 * `docs/inwentaryzacja.md`.
 *
 * `loading="eager"` and `fetchpriority="high"` - this is the home page's LCP and there is no
 * point pretending otherwise.
 */
---

<section class="intro">
  <div class="intro__text">
    <p class="eyebrow intro__place">Cholewianka pod Kazimierzem Dolnym</p>
    <h1 class="intro__title">
      Kwiaty balkonowe, rabatowe i chryzantemy <em>z własnej uprawy</em>
    </h1>
    <p class="intro__body">
      Zachęcamy do zapoznania się z naszą ofertą. Zamówienia można składać telefonicznie lub na
      terenie naszego gospodarstwa w Cholewiance pod Kazimierzem Dolnym. Wszystko, co sprzedajemy,
      pochodzi z naszej własnej uprawy.
    </p>
    <div class="intro__actions">
      <a class="cta" href={primaryPhone.href}>Zadzwoń: {primaryPhone.display}</a>
      <a class="cta-line" href="/kwiaty-balkonowe/">Zobacz ofertę</a>
      <a class="cta-line" href={directionsUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wyznacz trasę</a>
    </div>
    {
      /* Three facts, each one a restatement of a sentence this site already says elsewhere -
        nothing here is new information, which is the point: it is the paragraph above made
        scannable for someone who will not read it. */
    }
    <dl class="intro__facts">
      <div>
        <dt>Uprawa</dt>
        <dd>Własna</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Sprzedaż</dt>
        <dd>Na miejscu</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Zamówienia</dt>
        <dd>Telefonicznie</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </div>

  <div class="intro__figure">
    <Picture
      class="intro__photo"
      src={heroPhoto.src}
      formats={["avif", "webp"]}
      fallbackFormat="jpg"
      widths={[720, 1448]}
      sizes="(min-width: 1200px) 950px, (min-width: 700px) 48vw, 92vw"
      width={1448}
      height={1086}
      alt={heroPhoto.alt}
      loading="eager"
      fetchpriority="high"
    />
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* `auto-fit` with a 320px floor, like every other two-column block here: one column on a
     phone, two as soon as there is room. No breakpoint, and none needed - which column order
     results is decided by source order, so the text comes first on a narrow screen. */
  .intro {
    background: var(--paper-blush);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding: var(--pad-section) var(--edge);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 320px), 1fr));
    gap: var(--gap-columns);
    align-items: center;
  }

  .intro__text {
    display: grid;
    gap: 1.15rem;
    justify-items: start;
  }

  /* The village is named here *and* in the paragraph below, on purpose: an uppercase label is
     a poor sole hiding place for the most important fact on the site of a holding people drive
     to, and a label weighs less in a search engine than a sentence in prose. */
  .intro__place {
    font-size: 0.68rem;
  }

  .intro__title {
    font-size: var(--text-h1);
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    line-height: 1.04;
    max-width: 18ch;
    /* Without this the last two words - the italic ones - break as "z własnej / uprawy" and
       the phrase that carries the whole claim is split across a very short last line. */
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* The one italic on the site besides the masthead. --green rather than --sage: this runs at
     display size, but it is a fragment of a heading and not the heading, so it is held to the
     text green's 4.95:1 rather than to the large-type exception. */
  .intro__title em {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--green);
  }

  .intro__body {
    max-width: 52ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .intro__actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.7rem 1.4rem;
    align-items: center;
  }

  .intro__facts {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 130px), 1fr));
    gap: 0.9rem 1.75rem;
    width: 100%;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 1.1rem;
    margin-top: 0.35rem;
  }

  .intro__facts > div {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.1rem;
  }

  .intro__facts dt {
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-grey);
  }

  .intro__facts dd {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* The frame. `<Picture>` renders `<picture><img>`, so the ratio sits on this wrapper and the
     image fills it - putting `aspect-ratio` on the `img` alone leaves the wrapper at the
     intrinsic height and opens a gap under the photograph. */
  .intro__figure {
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 5;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .intro__figure > :global(picture) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  /* The only crop of this photograph, so it is aimed rather than left in the middle. The file
     is 4:3 and the box is 4:5, so about 40% of its width is outside the frame; 42% pulls the
     hanging baskets up into it instead of leaving the glasshouse roof there. Beats the
     `height: auto` that global.css puts on every img. */
  .intro__photo {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 42%;
  }
</style>
